      <floats-group id="article-24122.s28">
        <fig id="article-24122.image.f13" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Halo Sign Indicating Cerebrospinal Fluid Leakage. The halo sign is a double-ring pattern that appears when bloody fluid from the ear or nose, containing cerebrospinal fluid, is dripped onto linen or paper. Contributed by R Winters, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="IMG_0183" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-24122.s29">
        <fig id="article-24122.image.f14" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Coronal Approach to the Craniofacial Skeleton. This surgical exposure grants access to the frontal sinus, naso-orbito-ethmoid region, zygomatic arches, orbital roofs, and anterior skull base. Structures at risk include the frontal branches of the facial nerve and the supraorbital and supratrochlear nerves. Contributed by MH Hohman, MD, FACS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="CoronalApproach" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-24122.s30">
        <fig id="article-24122.image.f15" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Open Reduction and Internal Fixation of Midface Fractures. Because the fractures extend high up on the nasomaxillary buttresses, a midfacial degloving approach was used to provide exposure for placing 2 mm titanium mini plates. Contributed by SE Bevans, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="ORIFmidface" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-24122.s31">
        <fig id="article-24122.image.f16" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Left Orbital Floor Fracture, Transconjunctival, Post-Septal Approach. The image shows orbital contents being retracted superiorly to expose the bone, and a titanium mini-plate has been placed along the inferior orbital rim. Contributed by MH Hohman, MD, FACS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="OrbitalFloor" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-24122.s32">
        <fig id="article-24122.image.f17" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Pitanguy Line. This image illustrates the Pitanguy line, which runs from a point 5 mm inferior to the tragus to a point 15 mm superior to the lateral brow. This line approximates the course of the frontal branch of the facial nerve. Contributed by MH Hohman, MD, FACS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="PitanguyLine" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-24122.s33">
        <fig id="article-24122.image.f18" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Trigeminal Foramina of the Face. The purple circles denote the supraorbital notches, which present as foramina in 25% of cases (CN V1). The green circles highlight the infraorbital foramina (CN V2), whereas the red circles indicate the mental foramina (CN V3). Contributed by MH Hohman, MD, FACS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="TrigeminalForamina" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-24122.s34">
        <fig id="article-24122.image.f19" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Pericranial Flap Elevation. The pericranial flap can be elevated separately from the forehead flap if necessary. Contributed by MH Hohman, MD, FACS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="PericranialFlap" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-24122.s35">
        <fig id="article-24122.image.f20" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Nasal Septal Hematoma. This illustration shows a nasal septal hematoma along with the perichondrium of the nose. Contributed by&#x000a0;S Bhimji, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Nasal__septal__hematoma" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-24122.s36">
        <fig id="article-24122.image.f21" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Fascial Planes of the Face. This illustration depicts the facial fascial planes, highlighting the continuity of the frontalis muscle, galea aponeurotica, temporoparietal fascia, superficial musculoaponeurotic system, platysma, and the location of the facial nerve. Contributed by K Humphreys and MH Hohman, MD, FACS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="FascialPlanesTitle" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
